## Why This Is Asked
Real-world need to ensure token binding prevents replay across tenants and regions.

## Key Concepts
- DPoP proof binding
- per-tenant JWKS rotation
- replay protection
- token audience and scope mapping

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node-ish sketch: validate DPoP and issue bound JWT
async function issueBoundJwt(accessToken, dpopHeader, tenant){
  const jwk = await fetchTenantJwks(tenant);
  const valid = await verifyDPoP(dpopHeader, jwk, accessToken);
  if(!valid) throw new Error('DPoP invalid');
  const payload = mapToInternalClaims(parseToken(accessToken), tenant);
  return signJwt(payload, {aud: tenant.aud, iss: 'gateway'});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to handle cross-tenant revocation in a global cache?
- What are the failure modes for DPoP binding in roaming clients?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend multiple token types, implement a small, pluggable policy engine, and ensure tenant-scoped access with TTL caching. It also touches SAML adapters and middleware integration.

## Key Concepts
- Token normalization, per-tenant policy language, TTL cache
- SAML-to-claims adapter, middleware enforcement
- Edge cases: clock skew, cache invalidation, tenant isolation

## Code Example
```typescript
// Pseudo-implementation sketch
type Claims = { tenant:string; scopes:string[]; sub:string }
function normalize(token:string):Claims { /* validate OIDC/SAML and map to internal claims */ }
function loadPolicy(tenant:string, path:string):Policy|undefined { /* TTL cache */ }
function authorize(token:string, path:string):boolean {
  const c = normalize(token);
  const p = loadPolicy(c.tenant, path);
  return p ? c.scopes.includes(p.requiredScope) && c.tenant===p.tenant : false;
}
```

